{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Log","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.Basic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.ComplexDilogarithm"],"declarations":29,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.disc_neg23_neg7_not_int_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.disc_neg23_neg7_not_int_sq","kind":"theorem","line":608,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L608-L621","doc":"Discriminant squareclass disjointness between the `5₂` quadratic resolvent `ℚ(√−23)` and\nthe retired Alexander-layer field `ℚ(√−7)`: `(-23) · (-7) = 161` lies strictly between\n`12² = 144` and `13² = 169`, so no integer square equals `161`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.masbaum_c52_one_barOdd_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.masbaum_c52_one_barOdd_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":593,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L593-L606","doc":"Exact closed-form identity for the `n = 1` Masbaum cyclotomic-coefficient bar-asymmetry\nratio `ρ₁(q) = O₁(q) / E₁(q)` of `5₂`: because\n`q⁴ + q² − q⁻² − q⁻⁴ = (q + q⁻¹)(q³ − q⁻³)` and\n`q⁴ + q² + q⁻² + q⁻⁴ = (q + q⁻¹)(q³ + q⁻³)`,\nthe factor `q + q⁻¹` cancels identically, giving `ρ₁(q) = (q³ − q⁻³) / (q³ + q⁻³)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_oneLoop_barOdd_factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_oneLoop_barOdd_factorization","kind":"theorem","line":579,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L579-L591","doc":"Closed-form 1-loop (`k = 1`) GSWZ bar-odd coefficients in `K_{5₂} · ℚ(√−23)`:\nfor `b₁ = 209/2116` and `c₁ = −121/1058`, a factor of `23` cancels in all three basis\ncoefficients, leaving `(11 / (4 · 23²)) · (−10 + 7ξ₀ − 8ξ₀²)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_barOdd_resolvent_basis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_barOdd_resolvent_basis","kind":"theorem","line":566,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L566-L577","doc":"Closed-form bar-odd (`σ₁ − σ₂`) projection in the `{1, ξ₀, ξ₀²}` basis of `K_{5₂}`:\nfor any `α = a + bξ + cξ²`, multiplying `(σ₁(α) − σ₂(α))` by `10ξ₀² − 17ξ₀ + 18`\n(= `23 / P'(ξ₀)`) yields `((18b + 26c) − (17b + 22c)ξ₀ + (10b + 17c)ξ₀²) · (ξ₁ − ξ₂)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_saddle_diff_mul_deriv_sq_eq_neg_23","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_saddle_diff_mul_deriv_sq_eq_neg_23","kind":"theorem","line":550,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L550-L564","doc":"Combining `fiveTwo_saddle_diff_sq` and `fiveTwo_resolvent_sq_poly_identity`: for any root\n`ξ₀` of `x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1 = 0`, the element `(ξ₁ − ξ₂)(3ξ₀² − 4ξ₀ + 3)` is a square root\nof `−23`, placing `ξ₁ − ξ₂` in `K_{5₂} · ℚ(√−23)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_saddle_diff_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_saddle_diff_sq","kind":"theorem","line":542,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L542-L548","doc":"Along the Vieta locus `ξ₀ + ξ₁ + ξ₂ = 2` and `ξ₀ξ₁ + ξ₀ξ₂ + ξ₁ξ₂ = 3`, the square of\nthe conjugate-saddle difference `(ξ₁ − ξ₂)²` is the quadratic polynomial `−3ξ₀² + 4ξ₀ − 8`\nin the real root `ξ₀`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_resolvent_sq_poly_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_resolvent_sq_poly_identity","kind":"theorem","line":535,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L535-L540","doc":"Exact polynomial identity equating `(-3x² + 4x - 8) · P'(x)²` to `−23` modulo\n`x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_deriv_bezout_identity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_deriv_bezout_identity","kind":"theorem","line":528,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L528-L533","doc":"Exact Bézout identity in `ℤ[x]` inverting `P'(x) = 3x² − 4x + 3` modulo\n`x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1`: `(3x² − 4x + 3)(10x² − 17x + 18) = 23 + (30x − 31)(x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_cubic_x_of_z_factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.fiveTwo_cubic_x_of_z_factorization","kind":"theorem","line":520,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L520-L526","doc":"Exact ring isomorphism substitution between the Riley/SnapPy cubic\n`x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1` and the Landen/canonical cubic `z³ − z² + 1`:\nsetting `x = 1 − z²` gives `x³ − 2x² + 3x − 1 = −(z³ + z² − 1)(z³ − z² + 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_re_cLiTwoExt_eq_integral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_re_cLiTwoExt_eq_integral","kind":"theorem","line":441,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L441-L494","doc":"**`Σⱼ Re cLiTwoExt(yⱼ) = −∫₀¹ log P(z)/z dz`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_re_log_one_sub_eq_log_recipCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.sum_re_log_one_sub_eq_log_recipCubic","kind":"theorem","line":380,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L380-L414","doc":"**`Σⱼ Re Log(1 − yⱼz) = log P(z)` for `z ∈ [0,1]`.**\n\nReal parts, so no branch data enters. `y₁, y₂, y₃` are any three complex\nnumbers with the 5₂ cubic's elementary symmetric functions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.intervalIntegrable_log_recipCubic_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.intervalIntegrable_log_recipCubic_div","kind":"theorem","line":331,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L331-L353","doc":"**Route 5's integral converges.**\n\n`|log P(z)/z| ≤ 4` on `(0,1]`, immediately from `abs_log_recipCubic_le`, so the\nintegrand is a bounded measurable function on a set of finite measure. This is\nwhat makes `−∫₀¹ log(1 − 2z + 3z² − z³)/z dz` a well-defined real number, and\nhence what makes Route 5 a statement rather than a formal expression.\n\nContrast the series side: `Σₙ pₙ/n²` **diverges** (`|y₁| = 1.52 > 1`). The\nrestatement's whole value is that it moves the identity to a place where\nconvergence is elementary. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.abs_log_recipCubic_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.abs_log_recipCubic_le","kind":"theorem","line":307,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L307-L329","doc":"**The integrand's numerator vanishes linearly at `0`**: `|log P(z)| ≤ 4z`\non `[0,1]`.\n\nThis is what removes the apparent singularity of `log P(z)/z` at the left\nendpoint of Route 5's integral. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_le_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_le_one","kind":"theorem","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L302-L305","doc":"`P(z) ≤ 1` on `[0,1]`: `1 − P = z(1−z)(2−z) ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_ge_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_ge_half","kind":"theorem","line":293,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L293-L300","doc":"`P(z) ≥ 1/2` on `[0,1]`.\n\nCertificate: `z³ ≤ z²` there, so `P − 1/2 ≥ 2z² − 2z + 1/2 = 2(z − ½)²`.\n(The true minimum is `0.6151…` at `z = 1 − 1/√3`; `1/2` is the round number\nbelow it that makes the certificate a single square.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.landen_cube","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.landen_cube","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L267-L274","doc":"`z³ = −y` for `z = y/(y−1)`, given `y² = (1−y)³`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.landen_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.landen_sq","kind":"theorem","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L261-L265","doc":"`z² = 1 − y` for `z = y/(y−1)`, given `y² = (1−y)³`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.disc_eq_neg_23","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.disc_eq_neg_23","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L241-L253","doc":"**The 5₂ saddle cubic has discriminant −23.**\n\nImmediate from `disc_eq_symm` and Vieta (`e₁ = 2`, `e₂ = 3`, `e₃ = 1`):\n`4·9 − 4·27 − 4·8 + 18·6 − 27 = −23`.\n\n`−23` is the smallest `|discriminant|` among complex cubic fields. The\nreciprocal cubic `1 − 2z + 3z² − z³` and the Landen image `z³ − z² + 1` share\nit, being related to this one by rational maps. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:fivetwo-trace-field","label":"prop:fivetwo-trace-field","md":"quantum-observable-universe/appendix-qvalues/fivetwo-trace-field.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.disc_eq_symm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.disc_eq_symm","kind":"theorem","line":228,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L228-L239","doc":"The discriminant of a monic cubic, as a symmetric-function identity.\n\n`∏_{i<j}(yᵢ − yⱼ)²` expressed in the elementary symmetric functions. A pure\n`ring` identity over any commutative ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.powerSum_eq_pSeq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.powerSum_eq_pSeq","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L189-L212","doc":"**`Σⱼ yⱼⁿ = pSeq n`, an integer**, for any three elements with the 5₂\ncubic's `e₁ = 2`, `e₂ = 3` each satisfying the cubic.\n\nBase cases: `p₀ = 3`, `p₁ = e₁ = 2`, `p₂ = e₁² − 2e₂ = 4 − 6 = −2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pSeq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pSeq","kind":"def","line":182,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L182-L187","doc":"The integer sequence `3, 2, −2, …` obeying the 5₂ cubic's recurrence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.landen_image_cubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.landen_image_cubic","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L166-L174","doc":"**The Landen image of a 5₂ saddle root is a root of `z³ − z² + 1`.**\n\nFrom `y² = (1−y)³` one gets `z² = 1−y` and `z³ = −y` for `z = y/(y−1)`, whence\n`z³ = z² − 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.two_mul_re_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.two_mul_re_eq","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L142-L153","doc":"**The angle is determined by the real root.** With `y₂ = conj y₁`, Vieta's\n`e₁ = 2` gives `2·Re y₁ = 2 − y₃`.\n\nTogether with `real_root_mul_normSq` (`‖y₁‖² = 1/y₃`) this yields\n`cos θ = (2 − y₃)·√y₃ / 2`, the closed form used in the restatement — so the\nwhole sum rule depends on the single real algebraic number `y₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.real_root_mul_normSq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.real_root_mul_normSq","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L131-L140","doc":"**`y₃·‖y₁‖² = 1`.** With `y₂ = conj y₁`, the product relation `e₃ = 1`\nsays the real root is the reciprocal of `‖y₁‖²`.\n\nThis is what turns `log r` into `−½·log y₃` in the restatement, removing the\nmodulus of the complex root as independent data. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.powerSum_rec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.powerSum_rec","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L114-L123","doc":"The power sums `pₙ = y₁ⁿ + y₂ⁿ + y₃ⁿ` obey `pₙ₊₃ = 2pₙ₊₂ − 3pₙ₊₁ + pₙ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pow_succ_rec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.pow_succ_rec","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L107-L112","doc":"A single root's powers obey the cubic's recurrence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_pos","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L84-L99","doc":"**The integrand never vanishes on `[0,1]`.**\n\nImmediate from `recipCubic_eq`: on `[0,1]` both `(1−z)³` and `z` are\nnon-negative, and at `z = 0` the first is `1`.\n\nThis is what makes `∫₀¹ log(1 − 2z + 3z² − z³)/z dz` a convergent *real*\nintegral, even though the series defining `Σⱼ Li₂(yⱼ)` diverges because\n`|y₁| = 1.52 > 1`. It is the technical content behind \"Route 5 bypasses the\ncomplex-`Li₂` continuation\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.recipCubic_eq","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L77-L82","doc":"`1 − 2z + 3z² − z³ = (1 − z)³ + z`, over any commutative ring.\n\nA one-line `ring` identity, but it is the load-bearing one: it exhibits the\nintegrand as a sum of two pieces that are separately non-negative on `[0,1]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.prod_one_sub_mul_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwo.prod_one_sub_mul_eq","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoSumRuleIntegrand.lean#L64-L73","doc":"`∏ⱼ (1 − yⱼ z) = 1 − 2z + 3z² − z³` for any three ring elements with the\n5₂ cubic's elementary symmetric functions `e₁ = 2`, `e₂ = 3`, `e₃ = 1`.\n\nThis is the generating-function step: it is what makes the power sums\n`pₙ = Σⱼ yⱼⁿ` integers and identifies `Σₙ pₙzⁿ/n` with `−log` of this cubic. ","signatureChunk":0}]}